WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.461
Welcome to SplatWorks Educator.

2
00:00:02.661 --> 00:00:04.936
Start with a lesson you already teach.

3
00:00:05.136 --> 00:00:11.180
Before you open an app, name what students should think about and what you will collect as evidence.

4
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:15.923
SplatWorks is the ecosystem; DrawSplat is the whiteboard within it.

5
00:00:16.123 --> 00:00:23.176
Studio lets you search for a task such as making a chart, explaining an idea or practicing vocabulary.

6
00:00:23.376 --> 00:00:30.372
This course includes a field guide to all sixty-eight current destinations, but your lesson only needs the tools

7
00:00:30.572 --> 00:00:32.268
that serve its purpose.

8
00:00:32.468 --> 00:00:37.450
Our common example asks which place would suit a young plant on a warm day.

9
00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:41.557
The intention is to recommend a location using observations.

10
00:00:41.757 --> 00:00:46.989
Success means naming a location and supporting it with two relevant observations.

11
00:00:47.189 --> 00:00:50.818
A colorful board does not meet those criteria by itself.

12
00:00:51.018 --> 00:00:55.597
Ask what the learner will say, draw or write that makes the reasoning inspectable.

13
00:00:55.797 --> 00:00:59.829
Use this same question when you choose a tool for your own subject.

14
00:01:00.029 --> 00:01:05.914
Pause the video and locate DrawSplat, PlanSplat and one unfamiliar tool in Studio.

15
00:01:06.114 --> 00:01:10.194
Write one sentence describing the learning task each could support.

16
00:01:10.394 --> 00:01:13.481
Choose the smallest toolchain that fits the goal.

17
00:01:13.681 --> 00:01:19.612
Remember that research estimates about instructional strategies are not measured effects of these apps.

18
00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:25.118
The teacher still designs the modeling, practice, feedback and transfer task.

19
00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:30.459
Return when you have a goal, two criteria and a reason for your first tool choice.

